{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Isaac and Ishmael his sons buried him.</b> It should have put Ishmael first, for he was the firstborn. Rather, from here our Rabbis of blessed memory learned that Ishmael repented in Abraham's lifetime, for Ishmael was accustomed to yield precedence to Isaac; and since Ishmael was the firstborn, his being accustomed to yield precedence to Isaac — this testifies to his virtue and that he repented in Abraham's lifetime. And this is what they said in Bava Batra: from the fact that it is written of Isaac (Genesis 35:29) ״ויקברו אותו עשו ויעקב בניו״ — Esau did not speak before Jacob, and Ishmael did not speak before Isaac — hear from this that Ishmael repented in his days." "<b>And know</b> that the language of death in human beings is divided into six categories: three among the classes of the wicked and three among the classes of the righteous. Three among the classes of the wicked: מחייה (blotting out), אבדן (perishing), כרת (excision). מחייה is the harshest of them all, and it is the death of the wicked who have neither Torah nor wisdom and do not know or recognize the deeds of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His wonders, and they die the death of beasts and animals and their soul perishes with the perishing of their body. Of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 13:9): ״ונר רשעים ידעך״ — 'and the lamp of the wicked shall be put out.' For he likened the soul of the wicked to a lamp, and explained that it depends on his body just as the light of a lamp depends on the oil, and when the oil is exhausted it goes out. And this class has neither reward nor punishment ever, such as the generation of the Flood, who were blotted out from this world and from the world to come. Thus our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Genesis 7:23): ״וימח את כל היקום״ — in this world, ״וימחו״ — in the world to come. אבדן is the death of the wicked, such as the transgressors of Israel who died without repentance, and they are sentenced in Gehenna. As it is written (Leviticus 23:30): ״והאבדתי את הנפש ההיא״, and it is a death harsher than כרת. כרת is the death of the wicked who have transgressions for which they incurred כרת, and after they are sentenced in כרת and receive their punishment, behold they merit the Garden of Eden. Three among the classes of the righteous: <b>the first</b> is גויעה (expiring) and אסיפה (gathering) and ביאה (entering), which are all one, for all of them are said of Abraham: ״ויגוע ויאסף״, and it is written ״ואתה תבוא אל אבותיך בשלום״, and these expressions are said of the righteous who descend to the Garden of Eden immediately without any punishment. And so it is said of Ishmael (Genesis 25:17): ״ויגוע וימת ויאסף אל עמיו״. And from this our Rabbis of blessed memory said that Ishmael repented. <b>The second</b> is לקיחה (taking), and this expression is said of the righteous who enter the Garden Eden alive and did not taste the taste of death, in the manner of Enoch, of whom it is written (Genesis 5:24): ״ואיננו כי לקח אותו אלהים״. And so of Elijah it is written (II Kings 2:10): ״אם תראה אותי לוקח מאתך״. <b>The third</b> is מנוחה (rest), and this expression is said of the righteous and in the promise of the revival of the dead, for at that time there will be sabbath and rest, as it is said (Daniel 12:13): ״ואתה לך לקץ ותנוח ותעמוד לגורלך לקץ הימין״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:06.511409363Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}